# Target C-II — loaded vertices cannot occur internally on distinguished outer routes

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Status:** proof-only full-core C-II composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Depends on:** canonical Section 63 outer-route partition, C26, and the proved
`target_c_grounded_separator_and_loaded_nonarticulation_2026-09-11.md` loaded
non-articulation theorem  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The new long-side straight-event theorem leaves a formal branch in which a
near-straight internal route occurrence sits at a contact-degree-two vertex.  C26 then
makes that vertex positively diameter-loaded.  In the actual Section-63 outer-route
partition this branch cannot occur.

The reason is topological.  Section 63 marks one distinguished outer-face occurrence
of every positively loaded force-hull vertex and cuts the outer-face boundary walk at
those marks.  If a loaded physical vertex occurred internally in one of the resulting
intervals, that physical vertex would occur at least twice on the same facial walk.
A repeated vertex of a plane facial walk is an articulation vertex.  The existing
selected-stress PSD theorem proves that positively diameter-loaded vertices are never
articulation vertices.

Consequently **every internal physical vertex on every distinguished outer route is
unloaded**.  In particular the loaded degree-two branch in the current straight-event
dichotomy vanishes.

---

## 1. Plane-graph facial repetition lemma

Let `G` be a finite connected loopless plane graph and let `W` be one facial boundary
walk.

### Lemma RI.1

If a physical vertex `v` occurs at least twice in `W`, then `v` is an articulation
vertex of `G`.

### Proof

Use the standard block decomposition of a connected graph.  A vertex is contained in
more than one block if and only if it is an articulation vertex.  If `v` is not an
articulation vertex, all edges incident to `v` belong to one block (with the trivial
leaf case giving only one incident edge).  Every nontrivial block is 2-connected, and
in the induced plane embedding every face boundary of a 2-connected block is a simple
cycle.  Attached blocks can enter a global facial walk at a block vertex only through
an articulation vertex.  Therefore a non-articulation vertex is encountered at most
once on each global facial boundary walk.

Equivalently, one can cut the facial walk at two supposed occurrences of `v`: the two
nonempty subwalks leave `v` through different facial sectors.  Any path in `G-v`
joining those sectors would, together with a small circle around `v`, separate one of
the two face sectors from the face, contradicting that both are occurrences of the
same face.  Hence `G-v` is disconnected.  `square`

The statement is occurrence-level and allows bridges, repeated articulation points,
and repeated edges elsewhere in `W`.

---

## 2. Section 63 makes every internal loaded encounter a repetition

Let

\[
B_+=\{b_1,\ldots,b_q\}
\]

be the positively diameter-loaded set in cyclic force-hull order.  Section 63 chooses
one distinguished exterior occurrence of every `b_i` on the full positive-contact
outer-face walk and partitions that walk into consecutive intervals

\[
W_i:b_i\leadsto b_{i+1}.
\tag{RI.1}
\]

The hostile audit of Section 126 explicitly verifies that these are the actual
Section-63 intervals even in the presence of bridges and articulation repetitions.

### Lemma RI.2

If a physical loaded vertex `z in B_+` occurs at an internal position of some `W_i`,
then `z` is an articulation vertex of the full contact core.

### Proof

No distinguished loaded occurrence lies strictly inside `W_i`, because the endpoints
of `W_i` are consecutive distinguished occurrences in the partition.  Therefore the
internal occurrence of `z` is not the distinguished occurrence chosen for `z`.
The same physical vertex occurs at least twice on the full outer-face walk.  Apply
Lemma RI.1. `square`

This also covers `z=b_i` or `z=b_{i+1}` reappearing later inside the same interval: the
endpoint mark and the internal encounter are two distinct occurrences.

---

## 3. Loaded non-articulation removes the possibility

The proved report
`docs/reports/target_c_grounded_separator_and_loaded_nonarticulation_2026-09-11.md`
establishes, under the contact-minimal fully force-visible C-II hypotheses,

\[
\boxed{G-z\text{ is connected for every positively loaded }z.}
\tag{RI.2}
\]

Its proof uses the actual selected-stress PSD matrix.  Grounding at a loaded extreme
vertex and conjugating by a strictly positive exposing functional shows that no
positive diameter edge can join distinct contact components of `G-z`.  Every such
component must then contain an internal positive diameter edge; two components would
supply two vertex-disjoint contact paths joining alternating diameter-pair endpoints,
contradicting planarity.  No secondary lexicographic diameter-count assumption is
used in this loaded non-articulation part of the report.

Combining (RI.2) with Lemma RI.2 gives the main statement.

### Theorem RI.3 — internal outer-route vertices are unloaded

In the fully force-visible C-II core,

\[
\boxed{
\text{every physical vertex occurring internally in every Section-63 interval }W_i
\text{ lies outside }B_+.
}
\tag{RI.3}
\]

By C26, every such contact vertex has full contact degree

\[
\boxed{3\le\deg_C(x)\le5.}
\tag{RI.4}
\]

Thus a degree-one or degree-two internal route vertex is impossible.

---

## 4. Collapse of the current straight-event dichotomy

Use the notation of
`research/target_c_long_side_straight_event_dichotomy_2026-09-14.md`.
For fixed `beta in(0,pi/3)`, let `S_beta` count `beta`-straight internal occurrences
on the long-side routes.  Main proves

\[
S_\beta
\ge
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}.
\tag{RI.5}
\]

At a straight internal occurrence, RI.3 makes the physical vertex unloaded, so C26
forces degree at least three.  The degree-two population `V_2(beta)` in the main
tradeoff is therefore identically zero:

\[
\boxed{V_2(\beta)=0.}
\tag{RI.6}
\]

The local angular argument in the straight-event theorem then supplies a third
contact with transverse component

\[
>\sin(\pi/3-\beta).
\]

A physical vertex supports at most five outer-face occurrences, and one selected
extra contact edge can be selected at at most its two endpoints.  Hence the main
occurrence argument sharpens from

\[
2V_2+10E_\perp\ge S_\beta
\]

to

\[
\boxed{
E_\perp(\beta)
\ge {S_\beta\over10}
\ge {1\over10}
\left(
L_{>2}-{2d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}
\right).
}
\tag{RI.7}
\]

There is no loaded degree-two escape.

---

## 5. Current low-`t` numerical consequence

At the current floor, the long-side branch has

\[
{L_{>2}\over D}
\ge0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1),
\]

and

\[
{d_{out}\over D}
\le0.002883975+o(1).
\]

For the main choice `beta=pi/6`,

\[
{S_{\pi/6}\over D}
\ge0.3933368069590823665\ldots-o(1).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{E_\perp(\pi/6)\over D}
\ge0.03933368069590823665\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{RI.8}
\]

Every counted physical contact has absolute transverse component greater than `1/2`
relative to the corresponding long-side tangent axis.

This doubles the guaranteed transverse-edge alternative in the current main theorem
and, more importantly, deletes its separate loaded degree-two branch entirely.

---

## 6. Relation to the bridge-deduplicated long-side cohort

The present theorem and the bridge-deduplicated theorem attack different losses.

- Bridge deduplication produces approximately `0.396D` distinct nonbridge near-tangent
  outer contacts.
- RI.3 says **all internal vertices** of the distinguished routes are unloaded,
  independent of whether adjacent route edges are bridges.
- The straight-event argument converts a subpopulation of locally straight internal
  occurrences into strongly transverse third contacts.

A natural next composition is to localize the straight events to the nonbridge
near-tangent cohort and split their third contacts by whether they are loaded
attachments or internal unloaded-component edges.  That is a smaller and more
geometric frontier than the loaded-degree-two alternative left by main.

---

## 7. Scope

The loaded non-articulation input is presently a proved but unpromoted report, so this
composition is also proof-only and must not be promoted without an explicit hostile
audit of that dependency.

No KKT weight floor is asserted for the transverse third contacts.  The theorem does
not by itself provide a source-preserving diameter descent.  C-II remains open.
